  "notes": [
   "Fujichrome Provia 400X Professional [RXP]. Only copy available is the Japanese bulletin (page 7 plots, vector, clean lines). Date taken from the print code 'イメ技-06-02-FP' next to the reference number 163AR116A in the footer (no other date printed).",
   "The sheet prints the process as CR-56 only (the E-6 compatible Fuji process) and the densitometry as 'Fuji FAD-30S (Status A equivalent)'. Exposure: daylight, 1/50 s. RMS granularity 11: diffuse, 48 um aperture, sample density 1.0 (stated as '+1.0', i.e. above minimum density).",
   "curves: x axis is log H in lux-seconds as printed and runs -4.0 to 0.0 (this sheet has no positive exposures); y axis 0-3.5. r solid (plateau 3.20), b dashed (3.33), g dash-dot (3.48, lying just under the frame top at 3.5). g and b are about 0.09 apart on the shoulder and merge into one dashed band from log H -2.6; r runs about 0.09 below them until log H -2.4, where all three meet in one ink blob (D 2.3); below that the curves coincide within 0.02 and are traced alike. Curves start at log H -3.96 and end at 0 (D-min plateau 0.137).",
   "sensitivity: the y axis is labelled 'relative sensitivity' (相対感度, no 'log' and no minus values) and runs 0.0 to 3.0 with the peaks at 1.97 (blue), 1.73 (green), 1.42 (red); values are recorded as printed. The definition printed with it is the reciprocal of the exposure (J/cm2) giving minimum density + 1.0. Curves: b 388-499 nm, g 474-591 nm, r 555-689 nm. The end drops are near vertical, so the last few nm are only approximated on the 5 nm grid.",
   "dyes: spectral diffuse density of the separated dyes (max 1.0) as printed; numerically almost identical to the Astia 100F and Velvia 100F dye plots. Curves start at about 394 nm (yellow 396) and cyan ends at about 711 nm (D 0.29). Yellow is not traced beyond 555 nm and magenta beyond 660 nm where they join the base line (about 0.01). The upper-left curve at 394 nm is cyan, the lower one magenta; they cross near 437 nm (both about 0.035). Yellow/magenta cross near 496 nm, yellow/cyan near 525 nm, magenta/cyan near 590 nm (D 0.43)."
